# CANONICAL §7.3 fixture — Chamfer `angleDeg` (distance-angle chamfer, 2026-08-24),
# and the first canonical fixture to drive a Chamfer at all. It therefore covers all
# THREE chamfer modes: equal-leg, two-distance, and distance-angle.
#
# WHAT IS PINNED HERE, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY IS NOT.
#
# No geometry number appears in this file. The analytic volumes — the removed prism
# `d·(d·tanA)/2·L` and the `angleDeg: 45` ⇒ equal-leg identity — are pinned in
# `worker/tests/test_wp6_ops.cpp`, which can measure the solid. This is the same
# contract/corpus split the OffsetFace pair and `analyze_edge_op_range` use: NDJSON
# compares numbers exactly and is the wrong instrument for a millimetre.
#
# What IS pinned is the protocol shape a Rust or frontend reader branches on: that a
# chamfer carrying `angleDeg` produces the SAME `bodyEvents` and the SAME
# `elementMapDelta` as the equal-leg form it replaces (one `modified`, the picked edge
# `removed` — a chamfer consumes its edge), that the three refused params shapes
# (`distance2`+`angleDeg` together, 0°, 180°) are named `opFailed` step results with a
# `diagnostics[]` entry and NOT errors, and that the head is untouched across every
# one of them.
#
# EVERY ROUND RUNS AGAINST THE SAME HEAD. Each `ExecutePlan` is followed by
# `DiscardPrepared`, so snapshot 1 is the base of all six rounds and the rounds are
# directly comparable — the equal-leg and the `angleDeg: 45` deltas below are the
# same matcher, applied twice, to the same predecessor.
#
# `preparedSnapshotId` climbs 2,3,4,… across the rounds even though every one of them
# is discarded: it is a monotonic scratch counter, not a head fact. It is pinned
# precisely because a reader who sees it move might otherwise think the head did. On
# the refused rounds `lastValidStep` is `null` rather than the base step: these plans
# carry ONE op and it did not run, so no step of THIS plan is valid — the surviving
# prefix is the head's, reported by `historyPrefixHash`.
{"expect":{"v":1,"t":"hello","seq":0}}
{"send":{"v":1,"t":"req","id":1,"verb":"OpenSession","args":{"documentId":"doc_chamfer_angle_distance","documentRevision":0,"workerEpoch":4,"mode":"determinism"}}}
{"expect":{"v":1,"t":"resp","id":1,"ok":true,"result":{"sessionOpen":true}}}
# The subject: a plain 10 mm box, whose every edge is a 90° dihedral — the one
